{#
  IP forwarding for the fabric NIC, via the Node Tuning Operator.

  WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL

  OVN-Kubernetes enables IP forwarding per interface - br-ex and ovn-k8s-mpN -
  and leaves net.ipv4.conf.all.forwarding at 0. An interface it does not
  manage inherits conf.default.forwarding, also 0, and refuses to forward.

  That is the entire return path for an advertised pod network: a reply from
  the fabric arrives on the fabric NIC and has to reach ovn-k8s-mpN. With
  forwarding off the kernel drops it in the routing decision, leaving nothing
  on the wire - the reply is visible on the NIC with tcpdump and never
  appears on mp0 - while every control-plane signal stays green. The one
  direct piece of evidence is

    ip route get <pod ip> from {{ clab_fabric_prefix }}.{{ clab_fabric_router_octet }} iif <nic>
    RTNETLINK answers: No route to host

  EHOSTUNREACH, which the kernel substitutes only when forwarding is off on
  the incoming interface. A missing route gives ENETUNREACH, "Network is
  unreachable".

  WHY TUNED AND NOT THE NNCP

  nmstate grew per-device ipv4.forwarding in 2.2.51, but the build shipped
  with the 4.22 NMState operator (2.2.60) rejects the field - "unknown field
  `forwarding`" - and rolls the whole policy back when it does, taking the
  NIC's address with it. Tuned is the operator-supported way to set a sysctl
  on a set of nodes, needs no reboot, and is version-independent.

  WHY THE GLOBAL KNOB AND NOT conf.<nic>.forwarding

  Deliberate, and the opposite of the usual advice. A per-interface sysctl
  has to name the interface, and this NIC is hot-plugged - its name comes
  from PCI enumeration and is not guaranteed stable across nodes or across a
  re-attach. A Tuned profile naming an interface that no longer exists
  applies cleanly and does nothing, which is precisely the silent failure
  this is fixing. net.ipv4.ip_forward cannot miss: it is the same knob as
  conf.all.forwarding, and writing it propagates to every interface present
  and sets the default every later one inherits.

  The cost is that forwarding is on for every interface on these nodes rather
  than the one that needs it. On a node already routing pod traffic through
  br-ex and mp0 that is a small widening, and it is what the node would have
  had if OVN-Kubernetes used the global knob in the first place.

  include=openshift-node is not optional. The Node Tuning Operator applies
  ONE profile per node; a profile that does not inherit the base one replaces
  it, silently dropping OpenShift's own tuning.
#}
apiVersion: tuned.openshift.io/v1
kind: Tuned
metadata:
  name: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
  namespace: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_namespace }}
spec:
  profile:
    - name: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
      data: |
        [main]
        summary=Forward between the UDN/BGP fabric NIC and the OVN management port
        include=openshift-node

        [sysctl]
        net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
{% if udn_bgp_loose_rp_filter | bool %}
        # Loose reverse-path filtering. Off by default: the untagged fabric
        # session is symmetric, so strict filtering passes. Turn it on if
        # VRF-Lite or EVPN shows traffic arriving on one interface with the
        # route back pointing at another - that is asymmetry, and strict RPF
        # drops it silently.
        net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=2
{% endif %}
  recommend:
    # Lower number wins. The built-in openshift-node profile is 30, so 20
    # takes precedence while still inheriting it via include= above.
    - priority: 20
      profile: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
      match:
        - label: node-role.kubernetes.io/worker
